# tests/modifiers/scope.rxt — module `modifiers` (MOD-0.5c): where a global
# `(?i)`/`(?-i)` setting starts and stops applying, per the MOD-0.5a design
# gate (docs/dev/plan_completed.md [MOD-0.5a] ruling 1: "Ctx modifier state ... saved/
# restored where `caseless` already is"; PARSE-1's 17/17 smoke cells).
#
# Every block carries `features modifiers`: these constructs REFUSE without
# it (default empty enabled set) — that refusal is pinned separately in
# tests/modifiers/malformed_and_gate.rxt and belongs primarily to
# tests/reject/ (the module-name check tests/modifiers/*.rxt cannot make,
# since a `perr` block asserts only THAT a pattern rejects, never WHY).
#
# ORACLE SPLIT: `(?i)pat` at pattern position 0 and `(?i:...)` scoped-group
# form are valid python syntax and python-verified as usual. Every other
# block here places `(?i)`/`(?-i)` somewhere other than byte offset 0 of the
# whole pattern (inside a group, after a literal, in a second alternation
# branch) — python 3.11+ raises "global flags not at the start of the
# expression" for a GLOBAL (unscoped) inline flag anywhere but position 0,
# and has no unscoped `(?-i)` form at all ("missing :" — it requires
# `(?-i:...)`). Those blocks carry `# pcre2-only`; their match spans were
# measured against live libpcre2 10.46 with a throwaway oracle patterned on
# tests/probes/probe_mod05.c (kept in-session scratchpad, not committed —
# see the corpus05 worktree report for the transcript).
#
# THE D33 §9.3 RECORD: run against the MOD-0.5a-gate binary (vocabulary
# exists, `modifiers` has no producer) via
#   PCREC=<binary> bash tests/harness/run.sh tests/modifiers/scope.rxt
# every block's pcrec compile fails with "requires module 'modifiers'" —
# 13 cases, 0 passed, 7 distinct pattern-compile failures (measured
# 2026-08-12, before MOD-0.5b/c land). Re-run that command against any
# binary suspected of losing the wiring.

# ---- (?i) from its position; (?i:...) scoped, restored after the group ---

pattern (?i)ab
features modifiers
m "AB" 0 2
m "Ab" 0 2

pattern (?i:a)b
features modifiers
m "Ab" 0 2
n "AB"

# ---- (?i) applies FROM its position, not retroactively (PARSE-1) --------

# pcre2-only
pattern x(?i)ab
features modifiers
m "xAB" 0 3
n "XAB"

# ---- (?-i) unsets mid-pattern -------------------------------------------

# pcre2-only
pattern (?i)a(?-i)b
features modifiers
n "AB"
m "Ab" 0 2

# ---- (?i) inside a group is restored at THAT group's close --------------

# pcre2-only
pattern (a(?i)b)c
features modifiers
n "abC"
m "abc" 0 3

# pcre2-only
pattern (a(?i)b)C
features modifiers
m "abC" 0 3

# ---- (?i) set in one alternation branch leaks across SIBLING branches ---
# within the same enclosing group (the measured PARSE-1 semantics: option
# state is scoped to the GROUP, not the branch, so branch 2 ("c") inherits
# whatever branch 1 left set before the group's close restores it).

# pcre2-only
pattern (a(?i)b|c)d
features modifiers
m "Cd" 0 2
m "cd" 0 2

# ---- QUANTIFYING the scoping form is legal; the bare run is NOT ----------
# R20/SPEC-1's accept-controls. A tier-1 miscompile (found by the D27
# blinded writer) had `a(?i)*` compiling and matching a/aa/aaa, because the
# bare run's A_EMPTY node accepted the quantifier. The fix marks that node
# not-repeatable — and these two blocks are what stop the fix over-reaching
# into the SCOPING form, which libpcre2 and python both accept and which
# must keep binding its quantifier normally. The refusal half is pinned in
# tests/reject/ (11 gated rows, offsets included), because a `perr` block
# cannot assert WHICH diagnostic came back.

pattern a(?i:b)*
features modifiers
m "a" 0 1
m "abB" 0 3
m "abbB" 0 4
n "c"

pattern (?i)a*
features modifiers
m "AAA" 0 3
